{
 "name": "VAOPGCPREC 36-91, Mar 11, 1991, Application for Automobile",
 "content": "<p style=\"text-align: center\">\t<span style=\"font-size: 14px\"><span style=\"font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif\">Decision Assessment Document<br />\tVAOPGCPREC 36-91, Mar 11, 1991,&nbsp;Application for&nbsp;Automobile<br />\tOffice of General Counsel Precedent Opinion</span></span></p><p>\t<strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px\"><span style=\"font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif\">What the opinion is about:</span></span></strong></p><p>\t<span style=\"font-size: 14px\"><span style=\"font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif\">Does the present language of 38 U.S.C. \u00a7 3202 enable the VA to accept an application for an automobile authorized to an eligible veteran who is incompetent and where the application is signed by a spouse as payee?&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></span></p><p>\t<span style=\"font-size: 14px\"><span style=\"font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif\">Note:&nbsp; This opinion, previously issued as Opinion of the General Counsel 9-76, dated August 25, 1975, is reissued as a Precedent Opinion pursuant to 38 C.F.R. \u00a7\u00a7 2.6(e)(9) and 14.507.&nbsp; The text of the opinion remains unchanged from the original except for certain format and clerical changes necessitated by the aforementioned regulatory provisions.&nbsp;</span></span></p><p>\t<strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px\"><span style=\"font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif\">Impact on VBA:</span></span></strong></p><p>\t<span style=\"font-size: 14px\"><span style=\"font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif\">See M21-1, IX.i.2</span></span></p><p>\t<strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px\"><span style=\"font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif\">Summary of the facts and findings:</span></span></strong></p><p>\t<span style=\"font-size: 14px\"><span style=\"font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif\">See OGC opinion for facts.</span></span></p><p>\t<span style=\"font-size: 14px\"><span style=\"font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif\">A spouse-payee may file an application for an automobile on behalf of an incompetent veteran and such an application may be properly accepted and processed.&nbsp; Op.Sol. 273-52 is therefore rescinded insofar at it is inconsistent with this opinion.&nbsp;</span></span></p><p>\t<strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px\"><span style=\"font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif\">References:</span></span></strong></p><p>\t<span style=\"font-size: 14px\"><span style=\"font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif\"><a href=\"https://www.va.gov/ogc/docs/1991/PREC_36-91.doc\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.va.gov/ogc/docs/1991/PREC_36-91.doc</a></span></span></p><p>\t<span style=\"font-size: 14px\"><span style=\"font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif\"><a class=\"eGainArticleLink\" articleid=\"554400000014997\" articlename=\"M21-1, Part IX, Subpart i, Chapter 2 - Original Pension Claims Development\" href=\"https://www.knowva.ebenefits.va.gov/system/ws/v11/ss/article/554400000014997?portalId=554400000001018&usertype=customer&\">M21-1, Part IX, Subpart i, Chapter 2 - Automobile and Adaptive Equipment Allowance Under 38 U.S.C. Chapter 39</a></span></span></p>",
